DevOps Engineer Interview Questions (4–6 Years Experience)

If you have 4–6 years in DevOps, interviews are less about tools and more about design, reliability, and decision-making.

Here are real questions you should confidently answer 👇

🔹 Architecture & Design
1. How would you design a highly available CI/CD pipeline?
2. How do you handle multi-environment deployments (dev, stage, prod)?
3. Blue-Green vs Canary vs Rolling — when would you use each?
4. How do you design for zero-downtime deployments?
5. How do you manage secrets securely across environments?

🔹 CI/CD & Automation
6. How do you optimize pipeline execution time?
7. How do you handle pipeline failures in production releases?
8. How do you manage versioning and rollback strategies?
9. How do you implement approvals and governance in CI/CD?
10. How do you design pipelines for microservices?

🔹 Containers & Kubernetes
11. How do you troubleshoot CrashLoopBackOff in Kubernetes?

12. How do you manage resource limits and requests in production?
13. How do you secure Kubernetes clusters?
14. How do you handle Kubernetes upgrades safely?
15. When would you use HPA vs VPA?

🔹 Cloud & Infrastructure
16. How do you design fault-tolerant infrastructure on AWS/Azure?
17. How do you manage infrastructure drift?
18. Terraform state — how do you manage it safely?
19. How do you implement cost optimization in cloud environments?
20. How do you handle disaster recovery and backups?

🔹 Monitoring, Security & Reliability
21. What metrics matter most for production systems?
22. How do you reduce MTTR during incidents?
23. How do you implement alerting without alert fatigue?
24. How do you integrate security into CI/CD pipelines?
25. How do you conduct post-incident reviews?

🔹 Leadership & Ownership
26. How do you mentor junior DevOps engineers?
27. How do you handle on-call escalations?
28. How do you influence developers to follow DevOps best practices?
29. How do you balance speed vs stability?
30. Describe a production outage you handled end-to-end.

💡 At 4–6 years, companies expect you to think like an owner, not an operator.
